  "notes": [
    "Score is the engine's composite, 0-100: 35% win rate, 15% mana screw, 10% flood, 10% commander reliability, 10% interaction in hand, 10% clock, 5% dead cards, 5% own-fun. It is not a win percentage and the six decks are not playing each other -- each one is measured separately against the same pod of simulated opponents.",
    "winRate, screwPct, floodPct and commanderCastRate are fractions of games from 0 to 1, not percentages. avgCommanderTurn and avgWinTurn are turn numbers; deadCardsAtT8 is a card count.",
    "se is the standard error of the score across the six seeds. Two builds differ meaningfully only when the gap between their scores is larger than twice the combined standard error (2 * hypot(seA, seB)); every `significant` flag in this file was set that way, and gaps smaller than that should be read as a tie.",
    "The Master's target column already has the D5 and D6 Tuned swaps applied to it -- every upgrade ADD sits at target=1, every upgrade CUT at target=0, and fifty card notes date the edit to 2026-09-02. So `tuned` here is the published target hundred, and `v1` is that hundred with the Tuned swaps undone, one displaced card restored per Tuned add.",
    "Which card each Tuned add displaced was read from the per-card slot notes rather than from upgrades[].CUT, because the CUT list names cards that had already left the target hundred: Explorer's Scope, Wild Pack Squad, Fire Diamond and Rummaging Goblin were each bench-substituted out before the Tuned pass by a card that is still in the list, so restoring them would have left those slots doubly filled. The slot notes give a complete one-for-one map: nine displaced cards for D5's nine adds, ten for D6's ten.",
    "D1 through D4 have no upgrades, so their tuned build is their v1 build and is recorded as {\"sameAs\": \"v1\"} rather than measured twice.",
    "b3 is built on top of tuned. Six of D5's seven B3 swaps applied: Blood Artist could not, because the card it replaces (Prideful Feastling) was already taken out of D5 by the Tuned add Teysa Karlov. That row of the B3 sheet was written against the pre-Tuned list. Every other deck applied all of its B3 swaps.",
    "cost is the sum of the Master's own price column over the list, so it matches what the shopping pages quote. Every card in these six lists now carries a price in the Master -- the basics included -- so cost is a full total at the Master's prices rather than the floor it used to be.",
    "bracket comes from compliance-model.js: Bracket 2 permits no Game Changers, Bracket 3 up to three. Game Changer status is Scryfall's game_changer flag, the same field the app's own Scryfall client reads. All fourteen lists are clean at Tier 3; the B3 lists that show B3 are there only because they carry one or two Game Changers.",
    "The win-rate term no longer saturates. It used to be scored as min(1, winRate / 0.5), so every build above a 50% win rate got full credit and the score moved only on consistency; that was one of seven faults fixed in sim-engine.js, and the curve now keeps rising across the whole range. A build with a clearly higher win rate than another now scores higher for it. This is the engine's default, unbanded scoring, the same setting the repo's own power rungs are measured on.",
    "curve counts nonland cards only, bucketed by mana value, with everything 8 or above in the 8 bucket. A zero-cost spell has no column and is simply not drawn, though it still counts in avgMv, which is the mean mana value of the nonland cards.",
    "Every list was asserted to be exactly 100 cards with exactly one commander and no repeated nonbasic before a single game was simulated.",
    "Measured against data/master-v2.json as generated 2026-09-04T02:29:17+00:00.",
    "Regenerated on the repaired engine. Every figure in this file supersedes the previous one: interaction now requires an answer the deck can actually pay for, the win-rate curve no longer saturates at an even share, sacrifice is modelled, death triggers pay per death rather than every upkeep, the ramp reader recognises Cultivate/Nature's Lore/Farseek/Wood Elves and their kind, a land that enters tapped costs a turn, and a fetch land produces colour. Every score fell, because all seven handed out credit that was not earned; the ranking did not change.",
    "Regenerated by tools/sim/rate-decks.mjs, which builds all fourteen hundreds from data/master-v2.json and measures them here. Run on the engine as it stood before this repair, it reproduces eleven of the fourteen builds' published figures exactly and the other three (D3 b3, D5 v1, D6 v1) within 0.2 score points -- those three are the builds where a swap moved a card, and the residual is the shuffle seeing the same hundred in a different order. Every build is now ordered by the Master's own rows, so a rerun reproduces itself.",
    "Regenerated again after the engine stopped reading Scryfall's reminder text as rules text. Reminder text restates a keyword in parentheses and means nothing -- Bronze Guardian's Ward reminder says \"counter\" beside \"Double strike\", which set a deck-wide +1/+1 counter doubler on a card with no counter interaction, and cost D4 8.8 points of win rate once it was gone. Thirty-three cards across these six decks change classification; two smaller fixes ride along, a fetch land now entering tapped because the basic it gets arrives tapped, and a Treasure locked behind a condition inside an activated ability no longer counting as ramp. Scores move -1.07 (D4) to +1.49 (D1) and win rates -8.76pp (D4) to +2.28pp (D1). It cuts both ways, so this was not a uniform inflation; the ranking is unchanged at every rung."
  ]
}
